---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Site Down After a DNS Change? Here's What Happened" description="Site down after a DNS change: the five causes from cached old IPs to wrong records, and the recovery checklist in order.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Guide</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">Site Down After DNS Change</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">Site Down After a DNS Change?</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      You changed one record and the site vanished. Almost every time, it is one of five causes, and
      the first two account for the majority. Diagnose in this order.
    </p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2>1. Cached old answers (the innocent one)</h2>
      <p>
        If <em>some</em> users see the site and others don't, propagation is still in flight: resolvers
        hold the old value until its TTL expires. Check the
        <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">propagation checker</a>; when resolvers disagree, wait.
        <a href="/flush-dns-cache/">Flush your own caches</a> to test cleanly.
      </p>
      <h2>2. The record points at the wrong place</h2>
      <p>
        A typo'd IP, a missing digit, an old server's address. The lookup shows exactly what the world
        receives; compare it to what the new server should be. This is also where CDN setups bite:
        pointing at origin instead of the CDN edge (or vice versa) serves errors from a healthy server.
      </p>
      <h2>3. The target is down (DNS took the blame)</h2>
      <p>
        DNS only points. If the lookup is correct and the site is still down, the server, its
        firewall, or its TLS cert is the problem. Test the IP directly or check the host's status
        page before touching DNS again.
      </p>
      <h2>4. Delegation broke</h2>
      <p>
        If lookups return SERVFAIL everywhere, the nameserver chain is broken: a half-finished
        provider switch, expired domain, or DNSSEC mismatch (a stale
        <a href="/ds-record/">DS record</a> after key rotation SERVFAILs the whole domain). Verify
        your <a href="/ns-record/">NS records</a> and registrar delegation match.
      </p>
      <h2>5. The old server was turned off too early</h2>
      <p>
        During migrations, cached traffic keeps arriving at the old server for one full TTL window.
        Decommission it only after the propagation checker shows global agreement.
      </p>
      <h2>The 10-minute recovery checklist</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li>Run the propagation checker: agreement everywhere? Then DNS is fine, look at the server.</li>
        <li>Disagreement? Wait or re-check the record's correctness; never stack another change on top.</li>
        <li>SERVFAIL everywhere? Check NS delegation and DNSSEC before anything else.</li>
        <li>Recovering? Note the lesson for next time: lower TTL 24h before planned changes.</li>
      </ol>
      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/dns-propagation/">DNS propagation explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/what-is-ttl-in-dns/">TTL explained</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
